  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-02-08T19:10:38.904Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Right now with the current rating system to optimize avatars, it's simply a good, bad, worst system, which overally boils down to if you use one bad setting your avatar is bad. The major problem with this is if someone wants to add a feature to their avatar that puts them into the bad setting, by having their avatar called bad there's zero incentive to do anything else to optimize the avatar. As such, we still have a ton of unoptimized avatars as there's no motivation to improve it, they are just called bad. I wanted to suggest rather then a good/bad rating system, instead introduce a complexity system. Give specific weights to each element added to the avatar represented by a number, which this sum number adds up to a total complexity.  This means if you add that one weighted feature, you're still incentivized to do everything else to keep your complexity low. Then rather then filter by good/bad, you use a slider to allow as much complexity as you are willing to deal with, all the way up to no limit. If an avatar exceeds your complexity, its hidden. As mentioned this would help insentivize more optimization of avatars, because rather then being told your model is bad you're given a number, and you can play with all those suggestions to decrease your number as much as possible, learning about optimizations rather then just seeing its bad and saying \"screw it, its bad anyways\"",
